Article 6

(1) Women have equal opportunities without discrimination to obtain employment.

(2) Employers who employ female workers are obliged to provide protection that includes welfare, safety and health.

(3) Protection as referred to in paragraph (2) is: a. health protection/insurance;

b. health services at the workplace;

c. opportunity and facilities to express breast milk or breastfeed at the workplace at designated times;

d. obtaining maternity leave for 3 (three) months after childbirth; and

e. obtaining balanced nutrition for pregnant women.

(4) Employers who are unable to provide protection as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be subject to administrative sanctions. Further provisions regarding administrative sanctions as referred to in paragraph (3) shall be regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 5

(1) Women have the right to obtain health services in undergoing reproductive life and sexual life that is healthy, safe, and free from coercion and/or violence.

(2) Health services as referred to in paragraph (1) include: a. adolescent reproductive health services;

b. reproductive health services for women of childbearing age;

c. health services during the period before pregnancy, pregnancy, childbirth and after childbirth; and

d. pregnancy regulation, contraceptive services and sexual health services;

(3) Women have the right to receive health services in accordance with the life cycle of women.

(4) Health services as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (3) shall be implemented through promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative approaches.

